# Part Two

# The Sixth Majlis

A conversation between Atu-ji, Maryam Zamani and Bari Begum

Atu-ji: There, my dear! You have heard Zubaida Khatun's story. Look how her mother made her daughter so capable by educating her! Are we not human beings too? If we wanted, we could make our children into finer people than that. But where are we to find that kind of ability?

Maryam Zamani: Your Honour, if you will forgive the liberty, may I say something? How does it follow from this that mothers can teach their sons as well? The talk was about my Ahmad Mirza, and you went off into Zubaida Khatun's story. Teaching daughters is one thing, and bringing up sons is quite another; there is all the difference of earth and sky between the two.

Atu-ji: Bari Begum, do you hear what Maryam Zamani is saying? Do you too think that mothers cannot educate their sons?

Bari Begum: Atu-ji, she is quite right. The point was about one thing, and you turned it to another. We know this much too: if a mother is educated she can teach her daughter after a fashion. But teaching sons is certainly not a mother's job.

Atu-ji: Thank God that you have admitted one thing at least. Now the time for the other point is coming too. Just let me read out the testament that Sayyid Abbas's father dictated in his last hours; then you will have your answer as well.

Sayyid Abbas says: when I looked at the papers Amma-jan had brought and given me, this is what was written in them:

# The Testament of Sayyid Amjad Ali

After praise of God and blessings on the Prophet, the humble Amjad Ali submits to all those it may concern. Although no one knows when death will come, or where, yet this time, since I fell ill, my heart has been sinking of its own accord; perhaps my time has come. Let my relatives and friends not grieve over this, for if the days of my life really are over, it is a cause for great thankfulness that my senses and my mind are still whole at this moment: I can say what I have to say to anyone, and give what I owe to anyone. First of all, I thank God Almighty that He caused me to be born in the faith of Islam and showed me the path of His Oneness. Secondly, that He made me the son of such parents, who first took pains over my rearing and nurture, and then wore themselves out over my education and upbringing. If a thorn pricked my foot, it pierced their hearts; and whatever troubled my heart was a matter of life and death to them. Yet for all that love and devotion, in the matter of study they never once indulged me. They fed me well, clothed me well, gave me good beds to sleep on and good horses to ride; whatever I asked they did, whatever I wanted they gave; in short, they humoured me in every way. But in the business of my upbringing, whenever they looked at me, they looked with the eyes of an enemy. I know very well that the things I disliked as a child must have hurt my parents' hearts even more than they hurt me; but they chose to lay a stone on their breasts, and paid no heed to whether I was pleased or not. After that, I must also give thanks that God Almighty caused me to be born under the rule of a people whom God Most High has called in the Qur'an the closest of all in friendship to the Muslims. [TN: An allusion to Qur'an 5:82, which says that the nearest in affection to the believers are those who say "We are Christians".] Thanks to English rule, such light is spreading in Hindustan today that there is no longer any difference between night and day. The roads are so safe that where caravans could not pass before, anyone who likes can now go along tossing gold in the air with his eyes shut. Trade has become so easy that thousands of maunds of goods reach the west from the east and the north from the south in two days. To go to cities we used only to hear the names of is now like taking a stroll in the bazaar. If someone wants to send news a thousand *kos* (a measure of distance, about two miles) away, or to get news from there, it is only a matter of saying the word: whatever news you send, you get a receipt for it, and whatever you ask, you get an answer. [TN: The post and the telegraph.] Craftsmen did not know the worth of their own work before; they wore themselves out from morning to evening and at last, at nightfall, two or three annas would come into their hands; now everyone gets a fair reward for his labour. There was an old wrong that idle men always lived in pleasure and luxury while men of real work went about in disgrace and misery; it is by the grace of this government that all who had a right have come into their rights. Farming once depended everywhere on the rain or on water from wells; now the Ganges and the Jumna run hither and thither to the four corners of the land, and wherever a canal has gone there is always plenty. Besides this, apart from kings and nobles, the illnesses of the poor were never treated as they should be: in one place you had to pay the physician, in another you had to buy the medicines. Now government doctors go about treating people in every city, every town and every village; there is nothing to pay the doctor and no medicine to buy; whoever wishes can be treated, and take whatever medicine he needs. In the old days, in the first place, books were hardly to be had at all; and if they were to be had, it was at a very heavy price; and if one had them copied, a single book took years to finish. Now, thanks to printing, books and vegetables sell at the same price. Carriages that kings did not have before are now owned by quite humble people. Cloth that the rich could not come by before is now worn by the children of water-carrier women. Before, a person had to travel the whole world before he came to know what the world was really like; [TN: The sentence turns here to the present: "now", left unstated in the Urdu, is understood.] for the map of every country, most accurately printed, sells for the price of the paper, and anyone who wishes can sit at home and tour the mountains, forests, rivers, islands, cities and wildernesses of the whole world. Before, educating one's children was so difficult that there was no harder task; now government education has made this undertaking so easy that it is hard to remain ignorant and easy to learn to read and write. Before, the cleanliness of cities was something no one ever even imagined; now every lane and every street, road and bazaar is kept so clean that perhaps even the houses of the rich were not kept so clean before. Before, the poor cowered before the rich and the subjects before the ruler as a slave cowers before his master; now everyone has every kind of freedom: apart from a legal offence, let anyone do whatever he likes, and no one may stop him. If he is dissatisfied with one magistrate's decision, let him go and bring his complaint before another; if he does not get satisfaction there either, let him go and tell a third, and a fourth, until he can go and appeal at the sovereign's own court. Let anyone take up whatever religion he wishes. If any part of the government's law displeases him, let him object to it. In short, there are hundreds of other things of this kind. I have made this speech long here so that my descendants will know the worth of this government. It is the way of human beings that one who has always lived in ease and comfort does not value ease and comfort. Perhaps my descendants will not know the worth of this government, because they will never have seen any other age than this; but I hope that when they are educated and read in books the history of earlier kings, their eyes will open, and then they will understand that the protecting shadow of Her Majesty the Queen over our heads is a very great blessing and a sign of our good fortune.

After that, I thank my parents once more for marrying me to a girl of a very noble family. This wife came and drove the darkness out of every corner of my house. The bad customs that had come down in our family from the days of our elders, and the great superstitions which the men and women of our household held to be their faith and religion, she did away with so gracefully that no one minded; on the contrary, everyone was grateful to her. She treated my mother and sister in such a way that I shall remember the kindness till the Day of Judgement. The revered Khwaja Sahib had told her at the time of her departure: "In the first place, get along with your mother-in-law and sisters-in-law as far as you can; and if you see that it cannot work in any way, then ask your husband and set up house separately, and do not worry that people will speak ill of it or that your mother-in-law and sisters-in-law will take offence: they will take offence once and that will be the end of it, whereas living together there will be trouble every day. But never let your husband stray from obedience to them." I say plainly now that if she had wished it, I would have had no objection to separating from my mother and sister, for I too thought it a very sensible course; but she never let things come to that. My mother and sisters always sang her praises. Besides this, extravagance is another habit of women: in food and drink, at weddings, at births and deaths, they look neither forward nor back; they are always set on the idea that if one goes out to a gathering one must wear a suit of clothes that no one else has; that one must celebrate a son's *chhatti* (the sixth-day ceremony after a birth) with such pomp as no one has ever seen; that a father-in-law's funeral must be such that one's name is made in the whole clan; and if the house is sold for it, so be it, and if the household goods are auctioned, so be it. And although I never held anything back from her, not even my life, she always treated what was mine as an honest person treats something held in trust for someone else. She never spent a cowrie wrongly, never moved a grain out of place. Thanks to her I never had to trouble my head over the running of the house; indeed, if ten men had joined together to run my house for me, they could not have done better than she. My house would hold trading goods worth thousands of rupees for a year, or even two years at a time, and many of those goods were such that if you neglected them for ten days they would be ruined; but under her care nothing was ever spoiled. Then again, women have this malady too: a *doli* (a covered litter) is always standing at the door; they are never free from visiting their maternal aunt, their paternal aunt, their sister, their niece; and this is often the cause of discord between husband and wife. But with her it was always the case that only when I myself sent her to her parents' house would she pay a visit once in some months; and at a wedding, where there was some such close tie, she would go for a couple of hours because she had to; otherwise she had nothing at all to do with going about. Besides this, ever since my late father had me taught my few letters, I could not bear the company of anyone but learned men, and sitting at home made me so restless that Amma and my elder sister had named me *akal-khura* [1]. From the time she came, my being out all the time stopped of itself; in her company I no longer cared for company outside. She was with me abroad too, most of the time, and apart from thinking of my mother and sister I never once missed home; with her alone beside me, a foreign place seemed as good as home. Besides this, a debt of ten or twelve thousand rupees had come down in our family from my grandfather's time, and with the disorder of the household there was no prospect of paying it off; she managed things so skilfully that in ten years she paid off every cowrie of the debt. The house we lived in was always broken-down and dirty; she changed its whole appearance: summer, winter, the rains, whatever season's comfort one wants is there; someone who saw this house twenty years ago and came and saw it now would never recognise it as the same house. In short, how far shall I go on? I can never repay what I owe her. Now this is my entreaty to her: just as you have done me thousands of other kindnesses while I lived, the great kindness after my death will be to take such pains over Sayyid Abbas's upbringing that my soul will bless you. Although I know very well that your life will always be spent in good works, still at this moment I too owed it to you to leave you my testament in this matter. And this is my request to Amma and my elder sister: that after me they regard Sayyid Abbas's mother as being in my place, and indeed hold her even dearer than me. And if God Almighty grants Sayyid Abbas life, my first instruction to him and to his descendants is to strive as far as they can to acquire knowledge. This is a counsel in which the counsels of the whole world are contained; all the good things of this world and the next can come to a human being through the study of knowledge alone. Besides this, I will set down a few points of everyday conduct as well; if they act on them, they will be of great use to them in the world.

If you want honour in the world, learn the skills that are valued by the ruler of the day. In every age and under every government something new is valued. Those who think the skills of former times are still skills, and do not learn new accomplishments and new skills, always live in disgrace and misery in the world. Trade has been our family's occupation for several generations, but there is no need for you to earn your bread by trade as well; no, take up whatever occupation you see you have the ability for, and whose good and bad points you know well. Some people in service are always anxious about how they may get promotion, but they make no effort to acquire the ability on which promotion depends; this is a great mistake. A person should first acquire the accomplishment that makes him sought after of its own accord.

If some magistrate or some nobleman listens to what you say, never recommend an unworthy person; and if you do, set out all his faults and merits, however close a friend of yours he may be; otherwise, in doing good to one man, you will do harm to hundreds of worthy men; for once one thing you say turns out to be false, no one will believe even what you say truly.

Breaking promises, telling lies, taking bribes, high-handedness, exaggerating for the sake of fine speaking, getting things done by deceit, making jibes at everyone in company: none of these is thought a fault at all any more in this age; mind you never cease to count them as faults. [TN: The printed text reads literally "mind you never regard them as faults", which contradicts the sense; the translation follows the evident meaning.]

To earn your living you will often have to travel. Only on Fridays do not travel, because that is the day of worship: just as the Jews have Saturday and the Christians Sunday, so we have Friday. Apart from that, all days are God's; never hesitate to travel. People say that the *disa-sul* (the inauspicious quarter) lies to the east on Saturdays and Mondays, to the west on Fridays and Sundays, to the north on Tuesdays and Wednesdays, and to the south on Thursdays, and that one should not travel when the *disa-sul* is ahead of one. Never believe such things; sometimes a person does himself great harm through superstitions like these.

Perhaps you may come to deal in horses, or need to buy a horse for your own riding; then there is no harm in not taking a horse that is sick, or has some fault under the saddle, or is ugly to look at; but beyond that, never pay any heed to the scores of faults of markings that people have made up. In this age people think a farrier or a horse-breaker very clever if he can by some trick pass off a faulty horse as sound and foist it on the buyer; this is a very great fault. If you deal in horses, point out every single fault of the horse before you start bargaining.

Some people keep trading goods lying for years in the hope that they will sell when they can make double or triple the profit; those who think this way are always at a loss; and those who sell at once, for whatever they can get, suffer very little loss.

When you go to see a busy man, go when he is free; otherwise your visit will annoy him. One should not make friends with people who come without need and waste your time. Hold time dearer even than life. For the tasks that have to be done regularly, fix times, and not do any task whenever you please; you will see the benefit of this only when you adopt regular hours.

Some people think that being harsh [2] to their wives is very good management, and hold it the height of honour and self-respect to be always suspicious of them; such people make their own lives bitter and take the women's suffering upon their own necks. [TN: i.e. they will answer for it before God.] On my wedding day, at the very moment of departure, the revered Khwaja Sahib gave me this counsel: "Being suspicious of women is forbidden in the Hadith." I gained much from this counsel of his; you too must never forget it.

Never lose your steadfastness in times of trouble. Always rise early in the morning. Whomever you meet, mean to learn something or other from him. Do not sit about as an idler and a fine gentleman; and think of servants only as people who are also our helpers: when you are busy with some great task, have them do the small jobs; and when you have no great task in hand, do not think it shameful to do your own work yourself. People who have servants and attendants working for them often become altogether helpless by leaning on them. People of that temper always suffer trouble and loss.

Strive to learn new languages as much as you strive to learn new sciences. But first of all, set your own language right; then learn the language in which the books of religion are written; then gain mastery in the language of the ruler of the day. After that, whatever language you pick up a little of is not without use; but these three languages are very necessary to learn. Some people spend their whole lives learning the Persian language; this is a great mistake. It was needed under the rule of the Persians; that time has gone. Now one should gain mastery in English, and learn only as much Persian as gives grace to Urdu.

In short, whatever is good in the eyes of reason, never count it a fault, even if a whole world thinks it bad; and whatever is bad in the eyes of reason, never call it good, even if a thousand people praise it.

After that, this is my petition to my whole community: let them strive for the education of girls, and not cast into the dust the worthy gifts God Almighty has given them, and not waste for nothing the thousands of benefits that their education holds for them and for women in general; and let them understand that if a parrot or a mynah is worth more for having learned a few words, then what fault have women, who are of your own kind, committed, that they should gain nothing from learning?

So I have given all the instructions I had to give. Now I call everyone to witness my Islam, and I vow that, if I live, I will keep as far as I can from whatever sins I have committed until now. God is enough; all else is vanity.
